      "text": "### Background on the Book\n**Title:** The Deafening Silence: Non-Verbal Communication in Four Pacific Societies  \n**Author:** Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri  \n**Year of Publication:** 2019  \n**Publisher:** Oceanic University Press  \n**ISBN:** 978-1-83748-291-5  \n\n**Summary:**  \nThis 284-page scholarly monograph argues that silence is not merely the absence of speech but one of communication's most structurally complex forms. Drawing on twelve years of ethnographic fieldwork across Polynesian, Micronesian, and Melanesian communities, Dr. Tāwhiri develops a six-category typology of communicative silence. This typology includes terms such as *nooanoa* (held relational silence) and *faka'apa'apa* (respectful deferential silence). The book examines how each type of silence is embedded in kinship obligations, ritual practice, and everyday social interactions. The opening chapter features an extended vignette from Fakarava atoll, establishing the book's methodological stance that silence should be studied on its own terms, rather than being treated as a residual category in linguistic analysis.\n\n**Key Themes:**  \n- Non-verbal communication  \n- Communicative silence  \n- Pacific anthropology  \n- Ethnolinguistics  \n- Kinship and ritual  \n- Indigenous epistemology  \n- Typology of silence  \n\n### Background on the Author\nDr. Mereana Tāwhiri is an Associate Professor of Linguistic Anthropology at the University of Auckland and an affiliate researcher at the Australian National University. She specializes in non-verbal communication and silence-based communication systems within Oceanic cultures. Dr. Tāwhiri received her PhD from ANU in 2006 under Professor David Holton and has contributed significantly to the field with 14 peer-reviewed articles in respected journals such as *Language in Society*, *American Ethnologist*, and *Pacific Studies*. \n\nHer research interests include linguistic anthropology, Polynesian kinship, and silence in ritual contexts. Besides *The Deafening Silence*, her other notable publications include works on held silence in Tuamotu funerary practice and rethinking ethnographic methods in silent interactions.\n\nFor more detailed insights into her work, you can check the following profiles:\n- [University of Auckland Faculty Profile](https://openbooks.net/authors/mereana-tawhiri)\n- [Pacific Anthropology Network Profile](https://openbooks.net/scholars/mereana-tawhiri)",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
