arava in the Tuamotu Archipelago that elucidates her theoretical perspectives on silence. This vignette serves not only as a methodological foundation but also vividly depicts the lived experiences of individuals within their cultural contexts, connecting abstract concepts to tangible moments in everyday life.\n\n### Conclusion\nThrough her exploration, Dr. Tāwhiri contributes essential insights to the fields of linguistic anthropology and Pacific studies, offering necessary tools for understanding the role of silence in communication within Oceanic cultures. Her work highlights the need to re-evaluate communication models to accommodate non-verbal elements that are often overlooked in Western frameworks.\n\nFor further details, you can explore the [publisher's page](https://openbooks.net/books/the-deafening-silence) and a [review in the Journal of Linguistic Anthropology](https://openbooks.net/reviews/the-deafening-silence).",
